      "body": "## Motivation\n\nFixes #39453.\n\nThe DSA KPool decode kernel passes `block_tables.shape[1]` as `BLOCK_TABLE_COLS: tl.constexpr`. The eager decode path creates a different Triton specialization for each page-table width encountered at runtime, so ordinary requests can accumulate one compiled binary per reachable width. The bound is large for long-context models and the extra compilation occurs after the server is ready.\n\n## Modifications\n\n- Pass the page-table column count as a runtime argument.\n- Mark `block_table_cols` with `do_not_specialize` so Triton does not include the width in the JIT cache key.\n- Add a CUDA regression test that exercises widths 1, 2, 11, and 17 and checks that they share one specialization.\n\nThe clamp remains unchanged semantically; only the compile-time specialization is removed.\n\n## Accuracy Tests\n\nOn an NVIDIA H200 NVL (PyTorch 2.14.0+cu130, Triton 3.8.0), I ran the original and patched kernel with the same deterministic random BF16/FP32 inputs. The resulting K cache bytes, tail K values, and tail scores were byte-identical:\n\n```text\nbuf        equal=True  max_abs=0.0  nonzero_diff=0\ntail_k     equal=True  max_abs=0.0  nonzero_diff=0\ntail_score equal=True  max_abs=0.0  nonzero_diff=0\n```\n\n## Speed Tests and Profiling\n\nI ran four widths (1, 2, 11, 17) against the same backing allocation, synchronizing after each launch. On the same H200 process:\n\n| Version | Triton cache entries | Total first-sequence wall time |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Before | 4 | 2.7885 s |\n| After | 1 | 0.7870 s |\n\nThe result establishes the cache-cardinality reduction. The totals include first-use JIT compilation; subsequent widths reused the compiled specialization in the patched run (0.0002--0.0005 s each). Steady-state kernel work is unchanged because the width is used as a runtime clamp value.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Format and targeted Ruff checks pass.\n- [x] Registered-test validation passes.\n- [x] Added a CUDA regression test.\n- [x] H200 kernel/cache and output-equivalence experiments pass.\n- [x] No documentation change is needed for this internal JIT behavior.\n\n## Review and Merge Process\n\nPlease run the standard CUDA kernel CI for the added registered test.\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:x: [Run #40506193104](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/40506193104)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #40506193052](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/40506193052)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\nLatest PR Test (AMD ROCm 10): <!-- slot:pr-test-amd:start -->:x: [Run #40506193018](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/40506193018)<!-- slot:pr-test-amd:end -->\n<!-- pr-states:end -->\n\n## Independent validation on the available H200\n\nThe available H200 NVL was also used for an independent validation with the local source snapshots. The environment for the output comparison and cache experiment was PyTorch 2.14.0+cu130 with Triton 3.8.0. With the same seeded BF16/FP32 inputs, the original and patched kernels produced byte-identical outputs:\n\n```text\nbuf        equal=True  max_abs=0.0  nonzero_diff=0\ntail_k     equal=True  max_abs=0.0  nonzero_diff=0\ntail_score equal=True  max_abs=0.0  nonzero_diff=0\n```\n\nFor widths 1, 2, 11, and 17, the original produced 4 Triton cache entries and took 2.7885 s for the first sequence; the patched version produced 1 entry and took 0.7870 s. The totals include first-use JIT compilation; subsequent patched widths took 0.0002--0.0005 s each. The registered CUDA regression test also passed in the available SGLang environment (PyTorch 2.13.0+cu130, Triton 3.7.1): `1 passed` in 9.26 s.\n\n",
